Step two.

59
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:02,040
Three ways to get AI to do the work for you.

60
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,540
So, once you open up Figma Make,

61
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:05,680
We're greeted with a really clean interface.

62
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:07,800
And right in the middle, there's a big input box.

63
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:09,160
So, I've broken it down.

64
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:11,900
So, to get the AI to do the work for us, there are three main ways we can feed it information. The first one...

65
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:14,930
pure text-based commands.

66
00:02:14,930 --> 00:02:19,410
Basically, you just describe what you want clearly in text and feed it to the AI.

67
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:22,370
This method requires a bit more skill in writing descriptions.

68
00:02:22,370 --> 00:02:26,310
You need to clearly explain the layout, the features, and the navigation logic between the pages.

69
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:30,480
For example, "Help me build an app for collecting recipes."

70
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:32,360
The homepage is a card-based list.

71
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,600
Clicking on it takes you to the details page.

72
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:36,240
And at the bottom, there's an 'add' button.

73
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:39,680
Then it will generate it based on your description. The second method is to use a design draft as a reference.

74
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:41,120
This is my favorite method, by far.

75
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:42,310
and it's also the most effective.

76
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:43,830
So, let's say you have a designer on your team.

77
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:45,820
They've designed the UI mockup in Figma.

78
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:48,900


79
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:51,920
Then, paste it right into the input box in Figma Make.

80
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:55,320
And it will strictly follow the designer's specified colors and layout.

81
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:56,280
spacing, and generate the code just like that.

82
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:59,510
Because what the designer provides is basically a source-code-level reference.

83
00:02:59,510 --> 00:03:02,070
So, it's way more accurate than just trying to describe it with words.

84
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:04,930
So the page that's generated is pretty much exactly what the designer had in mind.

85
00:03:04,930 --> 00:03:07,350
The second method is using a reference screenshot.

86
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:10,030
But a lot of the time, we don't have a ready-made design to work with.

87
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:12,410
But what if we're browsing online and see an app we really like,

88
00:03:12,410 --> 00:03:13,510
and you think, "Wow, a certain page is exceptionally well-designed."

89
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:14,110
So, what do we do then?

90
00:03:14,110 --> 00:03:15,450
Let's just take a screenshot.

91
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:17,550
and drag the image into Figma Make.

92
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:18,490
and tell it,

93
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:21,030
Build me a similar product in this style.

94
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:22,710
It might not be as precise as the second method,

95
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:23,770
But the convenience is a huge plus.

96
00:03:23,770 --> 00:03:25,470
You can just take a quick screenshot and make changes.

97
00:03:25,470 --> 00:03:27,030
Alright, step three: the live demo.

98
